Beppy Colombo recorded particle bombardment at Mercury during a recent close flyby. Instruments

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:49.940
on the joint ESA JAXA spacecraft measured solar wind interactions with the planet's

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:55.720
exosphere. The data will support models of surface sputtering and atmospheric loss processes.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:01.100
Similar interactions occur at Earth, though Mercury lacks a global magnetic field.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.420
The spacecraft passed through regions where solar wind interacts directly with the planet's

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:11.860
exosphere. Scientists will compare these readings with models of surface sputtering and atmospheric

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:19.060
NASA's Curiosity rover created five new impact craters on Mars through its drilling operations.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:25.100
The drill holes reached depth that exposed subsurface layers not previously sampled by the rover.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:31.560
Mineral compositions in the revealed material differ from surface regolith and have surprised the science team.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:36.680
The craters revealed unexpected subsurface materials that surprised the science team.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.820
Analysis of the exposed layers is ongoing.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:44.980
The findings may revise understanding of how the Martian crust formed and evolved.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:53.280
The NASA JAXA XRISM Observatory captured a giant star's stellar wind being funneled onto its compact companion.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:58.680
The high-resolution spectrometer isolated the velocity structure of the inflowing gas.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:04.640
The captured material powers strong x-ray flares in this high-mass x-ray binary system.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.460
The observation provides new insight into accretion processes around compact objects.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:15.720
The data show how the wind is funneled toward the compact object before releasing energy

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:16.560
as X-rays.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:22.340
This marks one of the clearest views yet of wind capture in a high mass X-ray binary.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:29.020
French ISS astronaut Thomas Piscett noted that Europe's new space suit still required

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:30.700
